  schools but what we need to get to is  that plan for how we build capacity over  time and use relaxation as that as they  come through but concentrate on the main  objective which should surely be to get  as many children back into school as  possible and as soon as possible because  they are losing so much now through  losing education losing the wider social  interaction losing loosely using the  wider development and of course losing  their preparedness for the economic  opportunities of the future I know it's  something you've spoken out very  strongly consistently throughout the  pandemic are you concerned that without  more money without a bigger national  effort without more support teachers and  schools that some schools might not even  be able to fully reopen in September  well I think what we've seen shows how  important it is to have the delay is  happening again I think what we've seen  shows how important it is to have  planning the ticket planning that  everybody takes part in and takes part  in constructively and what we've seen I  think in the last few weeks has been has  been what happens when you've got for  example schools and groups of teachers  involved but other voices other factors  perhaps coming in too complicated to  enter frustrate them in their efforts to  get things open we've we've got we've  got a complicated position here and  closing a school is easy you just you  just say it's going to close reopening  it involves staff it involves school  leaders it involves cancels it involves  sometimes groups multi-academy trust  involve central government in court it  involves parents everybody in that list  has to be working together  constructively I'm wanting to get to get  schools open again or just one one  faction within that and it can help slow  things write down or construct can stop  them happening when they otherwise would  I think you have a persevering with our  own social distancing in these  interviews brings its own challenges